\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} summarizes TSR and CAR across conditions (50 rollouts $\times$ 10 tasks per seed). $\Delta$CAR is computed against the matched-codebase Vanilla baseline (v2: $0.745 \pm 0.024$, 3 seeds, for factorial conditions above the midrule; v1: 0.780 for supplementary conditions below). Random OCC ($^\star$) uses v1 code but is included above the midrule as a factorial control; its $\Delta$CAR is omitted due to the cross-codebase baseline mismatch. Statistical comparisons use paired $t$-tests and Cohen's $d$ against the Vanilla baseline.

\textbf{Interaction pattern between gradient flow and normalization.} The four conditions crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) show a pattern consistent with an ordinal interaction: gradient flow is associated with lower safety regardless of normalization type; whether normalization modulates the magnitude requires additional LN seeds to confirm (Section~\ref{sec:mechanism}). Full OCC with BN (gradient flow ON, BatchNorm) is the worst-performing factorial cell (CAR 0.725, $\Delta{=}-2.0$\,pp vs.\ Vanilla, $-$4.2\,pp vs.\ Detach OCC within the BN factorial row) and the most unstable ($\sigma{=}0.064$, more than $2.5{\times}$ that of other cells). Full OCC+LN (gradient flow ON, LayerNorm) also degrades CAR below Vanilla (0.740, $\Delta{=}-0.5$\,pp). Detaching gradient flow recovers safety: Detach OCC (0.767, $\Delta{=}+2.2$\,pp) and Detach+LN (0.765, $\Delta{=}+2.0$\,pp) both match or slightly exceed the Vanilla baseline (0.745). These results are consistent with the hypothesis that spatial label content does not affect outcomes: Full OCC (ground-truth occupancy), Random OCC (random binary labels), and Depth (egocentric depth maps) all fall within cross-seed variance ($p > 0.4$).

\textbf{BN + gradient flow destabilizes training.} Full OCC (BN) exhibits the highest variance of any factorial cell ($\sigma{=}0.064$, more than $2.5{\times}$ that of other cells): individual seeds range from 0.776 (near Vanilla) to 0.654 ($\Delta{=}-9.1$\,pp), indicating a volatile optimization landscape under the BN + gradient flow combination.


\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Overall results on SafeLIBERO Level~I (10 tasks). Mean $\pm$ SD across seeds; 95\% CIs via bootstrap. $^\dagger$1 seed; $^\ddagger$2 seeds; $^\star$v1 code. Factorial conditions (above midrule) use v2 code; supplementary conditions (below midrule) use v1 code.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Condition & Seeds & TSR & CAR & $95\%$ CI & $\Delta$CAR \\
\midrule
Vanilla       & 3 & 0.878 $\pm$ 0.032 & \textbf{0.745} $\pm$ 0.024 & --- & ---    \\
Detach OCC (BN)    & 4 & 0.908 $\pm$ 0.026 & 0.767 $\pm$ 0.024 & [0.744, 0.794] & $+$2.2 \\
Detach+LN     & 3 & 0.901 $\pm$ 0.016 & 0.765 $\pm$ 0.023 & [0.748, 0.792] & $+$2.0 \\
\addlinespace[3pt]
Random OCC (BN)$^\star$    & 3 & 0.869 $\pm$ 0.029 & 0.789 $\pm$ 0.031 & [0.754, 0.814] & --- \\
Full OCC+LN$^\dagger$   & 1 & 0.842 & 0.740 & --- & $-$0.5 \\
Full OCC (BN) & 3 & 0.854 $\pm$ 0.052 & 0.725 $\pm$ 0.064 & [0.654, 0.776] & $-$2.0 \\
\midrule
Depth             & 4 & 0.889 $\pm$ 0.042 & 0.780 $\pm$ 0.039 & [0.747, 0.812] & 0.0 \\
Cutoff@15K$^\ddagger$   & 2 & 0.883 $\pm$ 0.041 & 0.782 $\pm$ 0.031 & --- & $+$0.2 \\
Cutoff@25K$^\ddagger$   & 2 & 0.887 $\pm$ 0.061 & 0.724 $\pm$ 0.051 & --- & $-$5.6 \\
Cutoff@35K        & 3 & 0.933 $\pm$ 0.032 & 0.779 $\pm$ 0.017 & [0.768, 0.798] & $-$0.1 \\
Cutoff@45K$^\dagger$    & 1 & 0.896 & 0.764 & --- & $-$1.6 \\
Reverse Cutoff$^\ddagger$ & 2 & 0.839 $\pm$ 0.072 & 0.718 $\pm$ 0.025 & --- & $-$6.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Normalization--Gradient Flow Interaction}
\label{sec:mechanism}

The four conditions crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) form a $2{\times}2$ factorial (Table~\ref{tab:factorial}, Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}). Within the fully v2 BN cells, gradient flow degrades CAR by $-$4.2\,pp (Full OCC 0.725 vs.\ Detach OCC 0.767; Hedges' $g{=}-0.781$, bootstrap 95\% CI $[-0.108, 0.014]$, crossing zero and thus not individually significant at $\alpha{=}0.05$ given the small sample). The LN cells show the same directional pattern ($-$2.5\,pp), yielding a pattern consistent with an ordinal interaction: gradient flow is associated with lower safety regardless of normalization type, with the BN cell showing a larger deficit ($-$4.2\,pp vs.\ $-$2.5\,pp under LN) in current data, though the LN cells have $n{=}1$ each. We report effect sizes and confidence intervals for transparency; the small-$n$ factorial design prioritizes breadth across a 12-condition space over statistical power for individual pairwise tests. The consistent gradient flow effect---both BN and LN rows show lower CAR under gradient flow ON than OFF---provides the primary evidence for the ordinal interaction pattern.

\textbf{Ordinal, not additive.} Both BN and LN lines slope downward from Gradient OFF to Gradient ON without crossing (Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}), confirming an ordinal pattern. The BN cell shows a larger deficit ($-$4.2\,pp vs.\ $-$2.5\,pp under LN) in current data, though the LN cells have only $n{=}1$ each; whether normalization type modulates the gradient flow effect requires additional seeds to confirm.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{$2{\times}2$ factorial: normalization type $\times$ gradient flow. Both lines slope downward (consistent with an ordinal interaction pattern): gradient flow is associated with lower CAR. The BN cell shows a larger deficit in current data ($n{=}1$ for LN cells).}
\label{tab:factorial}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
 & Gradient ON & Gradient OFF & $\Delta$(ON$-$OFF) \\
\midrule
BatchNorm    & Full OCC (0.725) & Detach OCC (\textbf{0.767}) & $-$4.2 \\
LayerNorm    & Full OCC+LN (0.740) & Detach+LN (0.765) & $-$2.5 \\
\midrule
$\Delta$(BN$-$LN) & $-$1.5 & $+$0.2 & interaction: 1.7 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\textwidth]{figures/factorial_2x2.pdf}
\caption{$2{\times}2$ factorial interaction: normalization type (BN/LN) $\times$ gradient flow (ON/OFF). Both lines slope downward: gradient flow degrades CAR under BN ($-$4.2\,pp) and LN ($-$2.5\,pp), consistent with an ordinal interaction pattern. The BN cell shows a larger deficit; LN cells have $n{=}1$. Error bars: cross-seed SD.}
\label{fig:factorial}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Gradient Dynamics}

Gradient analysis (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis}, Appendix) examines the mechanism through which auxiliary heads affect safety. All gradient norms are computed with respect to the L1 action loss (action tokens only).

\textbf{Gradient orthogonality rules out direct conflict.} Action--auxiliary gradient cosine similarity is near zero across all encoder layers for both Full OCC and Random OCC, with conflict rates of ${\sim}$50\%. Action loss gradient norms are comparable across Vanilla and gradient-flow conditions, ruling out gradient amplification. Both patterns are identical for structured and random labels, consistent with the behavioral finding that spatial content does not affect outcomes.

\textbf{Mechanism remains open.} The observed interaction pattern cannot be explained by gradient conflict (orthogonality rules it out) or gradient amplification (norms are comparable). The detrimental effect likely operates through indirect modification of the LoRA optimization trajectory under a dual-objective loss landscape. CKA analysis~\cite{kornblith2019similarity} confirms that encoder representations remain intact under all conditions (CKA $\geq$ 0.95 for both DINOv2 and SigLIP), localizing the safety degradation to optimization dynamics rather than representational corruption.

Additional observations (per-task breakdown, depth comparison, and temporal dynamics) are in Appendix~\ref{app:additional}.

